findings=[
 {'ref':'F-074','title':'An AGL quota left open by one party of a shared transaction makes the next AGL trade in that transaction revert',
  'chainIds':[10,8453],'lens':'L3','assumptions':['A1','A4'],'effect':'degrade','actor':'pool-creator','severity':'low',
  'difference':'No chain rule is involved beyond the fact that carriers such as ERC-4337 bundles put the calls of several users into one transaction with one transient storage map. AGL 0x0000000000109de411eabc9f04906d79f06fda0e and its hook PoolAGL 0x0000000456b76bc801253ef2366664750f602840 hold code on OP Mainnet and Base (15,125 and 8,730 bytes), and cota() reads 0 outside a transaction.',
  'mechanism':'PoolAGL keeps one signed quota in a transient slot. afterSwap adds the negated AGL amount of the swapper to it. The token calls consumirSaida when AGL leaves the PoolManager, which needs a quota that is zero or negative and at least as large as the amount, and consumirEntrada when AGL enters, which needs a quota that is zero or positive and at least as large as the amount. Nothing resets the quota when an unlock ends. Step 1: party A buys X AGL in the official pool, which sets the quota to minus X, and closes its unlock with mint in place of take. mint moves no token (src/PoolManager.sol:322-329), so the quota stays at minus X. Step 2: party B, later in the same transaction, sells Y AGL. Its swap moves the quota to Y minus X. Its payment of Y AGL into the PoolManager calls consumirEntrada, which reverts with ForaDaCota because the quota is below Y, so the transfer and the unlock of B revert. The mirror case holds for a seller A who pays with burned claims and a buyer B whose take then fails in consumirSaida. Step 3: a party B that holds AGL claims can burn them and take up to X AGL against the quota that A left, without a swap of its own, which is the only case where a claim on AGL turns into tokens outside a swap. I read the hook source and did not simulate the two party sequence on chain. The token side of the calls is taken from the reading recorded under F-074 in cycle 4.',
  'impact':'A later user of a shared transaction who trades AGL has its operation reverted and pays its gas. No balance moves to the party that left the quota, which paid the 2 percent pool fee on its own swap. Nothing outside the AGL pool is affected.',
  'preconditions':'Two parties trade AGL inside one transaction, for example two user operations of one bundle, and the first one settles its AGL side with ERC-6909 claims. The first party must hold or accept AGL claims.',
  'mitigations':'Routers that trade AGL can read cota() before the swap and refuse to proceed when it is not zero. Bundlers can keep AGL operations of different senders in separate transactions. Core cannot change the behaviour of a token hook.',
  'coreRefs':['src/PoolManager.sol:322-336','src/PoolManager.sol:291-297','src/types/Currency.sol:60-75'],
  'sources':[{'url':'https://celo.blockscout.com/api/v2/smart-contracts/0x0000000456b76bc801253ef2366664750f602840','supports':'Verified source of PoolAGL: the transient quota, afterSwap adding the negated AGL delta, consumirEntrada and consumirSaida with their sign checks, and no reset of the quota.'},
             {'url':'https://mainnet.optimism.io','supports':'eth_getCode of the token and the hook (15,125 and 8,730 bytes) and cota() returning 0 on OP Mainnet; the same reads on https://mainnet.base.org/ for Base.'}],
  'evidence':'inferred'},
 {'ref':'F-027','title':'The block lists that gate OUSD and AMDc transfers on Base are each administered by one externally owned account',
  'chainIds':[8453],'lens':'L3','assumptions':['A4','A6'],'effect':'freeze','actor':'issuer','severity':'high',
  'difference':'B20 tokens on Base check up to three policies of the chain\'s policy registry on every transfer: executor, sender and receiver. A policy is changed by its admin alone. Ethereum has no registry of this kind in the protocol.',
  'mechanism':'A take of a B20 token is a transfer with the PoolManager as executor and sender (src/PoolManager.sol:291-297, src/types/Currency.sol:60-75), and a payment before settle is a transfer with the PoolManager as receiver. OUSD binds its sender scope to block list 0x7a, whose admin is 0x251d2711ebeb0a09fdb8992f5506f3d949175246. AMDc binds sender, receiver, executor and mint receiver to block list 5, whose admin is 0xec0f05c174e54fbf0fe16ad930a8afebce612812. Both admins are accounts without code. One updateBlocklist call from such an account that names the PoolManager makes every later take of that token revert with PolicyForbids, and for AMDc every payment into the PoolManager as well. The same call from an account that is not the admin reverts with Unauthorized, and no path in the registry source lets another account become admin without a stageUpdateAdmin call of the current admin.',
  'impact':'The PoolManager held 5,319,114.88 OUSD (6 decimals) and 32.15 AMDc (8 decimals) at block 61,314,282. After a block list entry none of it can leave by take, for any pool or claim holder.',
  'preconditions':'The policy admin, or whoever holds the private signing secret of that one account, sends one transaction. No token role is needed, because the policy admin is separate from the roles of the token.',
  'mitigations':'Issuers can move policy administration to a multi-signature account with stageUpdateAdmin and finalizeUpdateAdmin. Liquidity providers should treat the policy admin of each bound policy as a party that can freeze the pooled balance, next to the token roles recorded in F-027.',
  'coreRefs':['src/PoolManager.sol:291-297','src/types/Currency.sol:60-75','src/PoolManager.sol:349-365'],
  'sources':[{'url':'https://mainnet.base.org/','supports':'eth_call of policyId for the six scopes of both tokens, policyAdmin, pendingPolicyAdmin and isAuthorized on 0x8453000000000000000000000000000000000002, eth_getCode of the three admin addresses (empty), and updateBlocklist(0x7a, true, [PoolManager]) from an ordinary account reverting with 0x82b42900, at block 61,314,282.'},
             {'url':B+'policy/logic/v3.rs','supports':'require_admin compares the stored admin with the caller for membership updates, staging of a new admin and renouncing; finalize_update_admin needs the pending admin; built in policies have admin zero.'},
             {'url':B+'b20_stablecoin/logic/v3.rs','supports':'transfer_inner checks executor, sender and receiver policies through the registry before move_balance.'}],
  'evidence':'primary'},
]

cleared=[
 {'chainIds':ALL,'lens':'L3','check':'Exits, gas caps with measured numbers. Can the 16,777,216 gas cap per transaction, which is live on all seven chains, stop a swap or a liquidity removal that an ordinary account has not interfered with.',
  'result':'No. The deployed Quoters report the gas of an exact input swap of 10^30 wei of ETH, which runs the swap loop (src/libraries/Pool.sol:344) to the end of all liquidity, in the ETH and USDC pool with fee 500: 1,914,692 gas on OP Mainnet, 3,982,297 on Base and 10,163,544 on Unichain, against a cap of 16,777,216. A removal runs no loop (src/libraries/Pool.sol:146-238). CLZ returned 255 on all seven endpoints, which shows that the fork carrying the cap is active on each. Block gas limits read today are 40, 40, 60, 30, 280, 30 and 40 million.',
  'source':'eth_call of quoteExactInputSingle on the Quoter addresses of state/networks.json and eth_getBlockByNumber on the seven rpc URLs, 2026-11-23; https://docs.optimism.io/op-stack/features/osaka-on-l2'},
 {'chainIds':ALL,'lens':'L3','check':'Exits, tick crossing cost. Can an ordinary account place dust positions so that another user\'s swap can never be included under the gas cap, or so that a liquidity removal fails.',
  'result':'It can raise the cost of a swap and cannot block it. Each loop pass ends at the nearer of the next initialized tick and the caller\'s price limit (src/libraries/Pool.sol:344-420), so a swap that would cross more ticks than fit under 16,777,216 gas is done in several transactions with a price limit each. A crossing reads the tick and writes two fee growth words (src/libraries/Pool.sol:602-612), which by the gas schedule is at most about 50,000 gas when both words are written for the first time, so one transaction still crosses several hundred initialized ticks. The party that initializes a tick pays more gas for it than a swapper pays to cross it. modifyLiquidity touches the two ticks of the position and no others (src/libraries/Pool.sol:146-238), so dust on other ticks does not change the cost of a removal. On Unichain a full sweep is at 61 percent of the cap today, so added dust ticks would force a full sweep of that pool into two transactions.',
  'source':'src/libraries/Pool.sol:146-238 and 344-420; Quoter measurements in raw/quoter_gas.json of this unit\'s work directory'},
 {'chainIds':ALL,'lens':'L2','check':'Exits under a censoring or halted sequencer. Is the forced path through L1 deposits still open for a position owner after Karst and Azul, and can an ordinary account close it.',
  'result':'The path is open and its cost can be raised only by buying deposit gas on L1. Deposits are exempt from the 16,777,216 gas cap and are limited to 20,000,000 gas per L1 block, and a block that omits them is invalid. On Base nodes derive the missing blocks themselves after a sequencing window of 3,600 L1 blocks. A removal through the deployed PositionManager needs a few hundred thousand gas. An account that fills the deposit gas market pays L1 fees for every block it fills. This matters only when the sequencer already censors, which needs the operator.',
  'source':'https://docs.optimism.io/notices/archive/upgrade-19 ; https://docs.base.org/specifications/security/reliability-and-liveness.md ; https://docs.base.org/specifications/transactions/throughput-and-limits.md'},
 {'chainIds':[8453],'lens':'L4','check':'Exits and payouts, special recipients on Base. What the factory, the policy registry, the activation registry, the transaction context precompile, the nonce manager precompile, two created B20 tokens and one B20 shaped address without a token answer to the sixteen call shapes when the PoolManager is the caller of an inner frame.',
  'result':'A forwarding stub placed at the PoolManager address by state override made each call. The five singleton precompiles revert on every shape, and on empty calldata with 1 wei they revert with 0x6fb1b0e9, so a native take that names one of them reverts and no value is stranded. OUSD and AMDc answer transfer and balanceOf and revert on the rest. The uncreated B20 address reverts on every shape with empty data, so it does not behave as a codeless currency. Two control addresses without code next to the precompiles returned success with empty data on every shape.',
  'source':'eth_call with state overrides on https://mainnet.base.org/ near block 61,313,000, script sweep_base.py and result raw/sweep_base_special.json in this unit\'s work directory'},
 {'chainIds':[8453],'lens':'L4','check':'Lead L-484, part one: every code path of the B20 stablecoin and asset logic v3, the factory and the policy registry at v1.5.6 that writes a balance or an allowance, and who may call it.',
  'result':'Balances are written in move_balance, burn_inner and mint. move_balance is reached from transfer with the caller as source, from transferFrom after the allowance of the source for the caller is checked, and from seizeWithMemo after the Seize role check. burn_inner needs the Burn role on the caller\'s own balance or the BurnBlocked role on a blocked account. mint needs the Mint role. Allowances are written by approve for the caller and by permit for the recovered signer, which must equal the owner. The asset variant adds batchMint (Mint role) and announce, which needs the operator role and then routes its inner calls with the same caller and the same privilege flag. The privilege flag that skips policy and role checks is true only inside the factory\'s creation of a token, and the factory builds the token object from the address it has just computed and checked to hold no code, so the flag cannot reach an existing token. The credited account is the named recipient: the recipient strategy returns the given address or an error for zero and for the token itself. A caller without a role can therefore change another holder\'s balance only by paying into it.',
  'source':B+'b20_stablecoin/logic/v3.rs ; '+B+'b20_stablecoin/dispatch.rs ; '+B+'b20_asset/dispatch.rs ; '+B+'b20_factory/logic/v1.rs ; '+B+'common/ops/credit_recipient.rs'},
 {'chainIds':[8453],'lens':'L4','check':'Lead L-484, parts two and three: can a caller without a role change whether the PoolManager passes a policy that a pooled token is bound to, and what do the unprivileged paths answer on chain for OUSD with the PoolManager as holder.',
  'result':'No. updatePolicy on a token needs the default admin role and a policy that exists. Membership, composite children and the admin of a policy change only on a call of its admin, and the built in policies 0 and 1 have admin zero. From an ordinary account at block 61,314,282: updateBlocklist(0x7a, true, [PoolManager]), stageUpdateAdmin and renounceAdmin revert with Unauthorized, finalizeUpdateAdmin with NoPendingAdmin, updateAllowlist and updateComposite with IncompatiblePolicyType, and the inverted id of 0x7a with PolicyNotFound. On OUSD, transferFrom(PoolManager, caller, 1) reverts with InsufficientAllowance, burnBlocked, seizeWithMemo, mint, updatePolicy and pause with AccessControlUnauthorizedAccount, and permit with owner PoolManager and a made up signature with InvalidSigner. allowance(PoolManager, caller), allowance(PoolManager, factory) and nonces(PoolManager) are 0. createPolicy succeeds for anyone and returns a new id that no token is bound to.',
  'source':'eth_call on https://mainnet.base.org/ at block 61,314,282, results raw/b20_unprivileged_paths.json and raw/b20_policies.json in this unit\'s work directory; '+B+'policy/logic/v3.rs'},
 {'chainIds':[8453],'lens':'L2','check':'Known weakness: the Base block production halts of 25 and 26 June 2026, caused by journal state kept after a rejected transaction. Could an ordinary account use that fault, or one like it, to carry the unlock flag, a delta or a synced currency of the PoolManager into another transaction (assumption A1).',
  'result':'No funds path. The state that leaked was the warm set of a transaction that failed validation, before any code ran, so it held no transient writes of the PoolManager. A block built on a contaminated journal is rejected by every node that executes it from a clean journal, which is what halted the chain, so a leaked value cannot become canonical state. The fault let whoever sent the rejected transaction stop block production for about two hours, which delays exits and takes nothing. At v1.5.6 catch_error calls discard_tx for every error that is not a failed deposit, and a failed deposit reverts to the first checkpoint and commits.',
  'source':'https://www.base.org/blog/postmortem-june-25th-block-production-outage.md ; https://github.com/base/base/pull/3806 ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/base/base/v1.5.6/crates/common/evm/src/handler.rs'},
 {'chainIds':ALL,'lens':'L2','check':'Known weakness: op-reth and kona accepted deposit transactions written as a bare RLP body without the 0x7E type byte (fixed by optimism pull request 22778, released in op-reth v2.4.3). Could an ordinary account use it to run a call with a sender or a value that nobody authorised.',
  'result':'No for an ordinary account. The pull request describes a bypass of the leading-byte gates of derivation, which read batches. Batches are written by the batcher of the chain, so the party that could have placed an unsigned deposit, with a chosen sender and a mint, is the operator. Unichain, Ink and Soneium report op-reth v2.9.0-rc.1, which is later than v2.4.3. Zora reports conduit-op-reth v2.5.1, which is later by number only, since the Conduit build has its own numbering. The page lists four referencing pull requests numbered 4970 to 4973 with the title fix(consensus): reject non-canonical transaction encodings and does not name their repository, so the state of the Base client is not confirmed. I did not confirm the fix in the build of the OP Mainnet sequencer, which reports no version, or in the op-reth revision that World Chain v2.8.2 pins, and I did not read the decoder itself.',
  'source':'https://github.com/ethereum-optimism/optimism/pull/22778 ; web3_clientVersion on the seven rpc URLs of state/networks.json on 2026-11-23'},
 {'chainIds':ALL,'lens':'L1','check':'Known weakness: the reth trie iterator fault of November 2026 (wrong incremental state roots, stalls, wrong eth_getProof answers). Does it reach balances or pool state of the PoolManager.',
  'result':'No. The fault is in the computation of the state root and of proofs, and execution of calls is not affected. A node with a wrong root stalls or disagrees with the others. The exposed parties are bridges and services that trust eth_getProof. Base v1.5.6 and op-reth v2.9.0-rc.1 are later than the fixed releases v1.5.3 and v2.8.1. The Zora build (conduit-op-reth v2.5.1), the World Chain build (v2.8.2 of its own numbering) and the OP Mainnet build could not be matched to a fixed release.',
  'source':'https://www.0xcert.com/advisory/0xCERT-2026-0317'},
 {'chainIds':[8453],'lens':'L4','check':'Known weaknesses: the four Cantina reviews of the Base precompiles (Beryl B20, Beryl precompile storage, Cobalt B20, Denim B20 v3). For each finding that touches calls the PoolManager makes or receives: could an ordinary account use it against the PoolManager balance.',
  'result':'None could. The high findings are an abort of execution on oversized pairing input and a changed access order in a frozen logic version, both consensus faults and both fixed. Storage writes of native precompiles skipped the 2,300 gas stipend check of EIP-2200. The PoolManager forwards all gas on every call it makes (src/types/Currency.sol:48 and 73, src/libraries/Hooks.sol:134) and holds no guard that relies on the stipend, so this changes nothing for it. Value sent to nonpayable factory and activation registry selectors was stranded and is now rejected, which the sweep of this cycle confirms. permit accepted high-s signatures, which still need the owner\'s own signature. Calls to B20 shaped addresses without a token revert before any token logic, which the auditors and the sweep both state. Unknown block list ids authorize every account and unknown allow list ids authorize none, and updatePolicy refuses an id that does not exist. The remaining items concern gas metering, refunds, metrics, string storage and test coverage.',
  'source':'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/base/base/main/audits/beryl/beryl-b20.pdf ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/base/base/main/audits/beryl/beryl-precompiles-storage.pdf ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/base/base/main/audits/cobalt/cobalt-b20.pdf ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/base/base/main/audits/denim/denim-b20-v3.pdf'},
 {'chainIds':[8453],'lens':'L1','check':'Known weaknesses: the Immunefi audit competition for the Azul upgrade of Base (April and May 2026) and the Cantina review of 200 ms blocks.',
  'result':'The published critical and high reports are unbounded snappy decompression in the gossip layer, engine and batcher stalls, a consensus split in cached flashblock execution, and wrong precompile rules in the ZK proof executor that would let a proof attest to an invalid L2 state root. The first three groups stop or split nodes. The last group concerns what the L1 proof contracts accept and so the funds in the L1 bridge. None gives an account on L2 a way to change a PoolManager balance, a delta or a claim. The 200 ms block review has three low findings about stale lookups after a reorg and proof history.',
  'source':'https://reports.immunefi.com/base ; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/base/base/main/audits/denim/denim-200ms-blocks.pdf'},
 {'chainIds':[10,130,7777777,57073,1868],'lens':'L4','check':'Known weaknesses of the OP Stack in 2026 outside the execution client: the three derivation differences in the kona fallback proof program disclosed on 1 April 2026, and the changes Karst made to predeploy upgrades and to the pairing precompile.',
  'result':'The kona findings concern which L2 chain a fault proof accepts, and the notice states that no chain used kona as its respected proof at the time. They bear on withdrawals through the L1 bridge. Karst lets governance upgrade predeploys through the L2 Contract Manager, which adds no caller that an ordinary account controls, and lowers the pairing input limit to 300 pairs. The PoolManager bytecode calls no precompile.',
  'source':'https://gov.optimism.io/t/vulnerability-in-kona-fallback-proof-system/10650 ; https://docs.optimism.io/op-stack/protocol/hardforks/karst'},
 {'chainIds':[480],'lens':'L2','check':'Known weaknesses of World Chain: the Nethermind reviews of the PBH entry point contracts and of the block builder.',
  'result':'The findings are about who may consume a nullifier hash, a missing length check between user operations and proofs, a skipped gas adjustment in the builder and stale PBH limits. They decide which transactions get priority blockspace and how many. A PBH bundle is an ordinary ERC-4337 bundle in one transaction, which earlier cycles walked under assumption A1.',
  'source':'https://www.nethermind.io/audits/world-chain-pbh ; https://www.nethermind.io/audits/world-chain-builder'},
 {'chainIds':[7777777],'lens':'L3','check':'Lead L-306 for Zora: does a take of the whole PoolManager balance that is repaid in the same transaction leave any storage changed in the token or in a contract it calls.',
  'result':'No, for all 41 tokens with a balance. Method without a trace endpoint: eth_createAccessList of the transfer out lists every slot the token touches. One eth_simulateV1 request then runs the transfer out and back through forwarding stubs in a first block and, in a second block, replaces the code of each touched contract with a stub that returns the listed slots. All slots read the values that eth_getStorageAt gave before. The one way run changed exactly the two balance slots for every token. The method sees only slots that the transfer out touches.',
  'source':'eth_createAccessList, eth_getStorageAt and eth_simulateV1 on https://rpc.zora.energy/ on 2026-11-23, script simdiff.py and result raw/simdiff_7777777.json in this unit\'s work directory'},
 {'chainIds':[1868],'lens':'L3','check':'Lead L-306 for Soneium: the same round trip and one way state diffs for the pooled tokens.',
  'result':'70 tokens were tested. 60 leave no slot changed after a round trip. Six lending receipt tokens (Aave and Sake markets, for example aSoneWETH 0x1bd45cc20ce61be344a64218e6ade01e72b08f39) update the stored index of the two accounts. CBLT writes vote checkpoints. SHIROKE and Vaulted WIIZZZAARDS deliver 98 and 99 percent, so the fresh address cannot send the full amount back. None of these is a count or a volume that a repaid take can use up. Resonance refuses every transfer, so its 11,952 units cannot leave the PoolManager. 18 listed tokens were not reached in the time allowed.',
  'source':'eth_createAccessList and eth_simulateV1 on https://soneium-rpc.publicnode.com on 2026-11-23, result raw/simdiff_1868.json in this unit\'s work directory; https://soneium.blockscout.com/api/v2/smart-contracts/0xa0c458aa0695ad09bcd42bcabc198819cd271fc6'},
 {'chainIds':[480],'lens':'L3','check':'Lead L-306 for World Chain: round trip and one way state diffs by debug_traceCall with the prestate tracer for the tokens on the explorer holdings list.',
  'result':'457 tokens were traced. 444 leave no storage changed after a round trip. Of the 13 others, nine show the pattern of a transfer tax, where the return leg fails for lack of balance (checked for Cappuccino at 99 percent, DNA at 94 percent and MUPPETS at 99 percent, and assumed for six more from the same three slot pattern), przPOOL writes its time weighted balance ledger, Unishop.ai adds the fresh address to a holder registry in 0x30c72481f37d5c639dee5c0f1dea968e9605d559, BB adds to two counters in an unverified contract, and PIZZA 0xa1f70fbc0ccb017f0c2e10cd4581c543082fcdcc lets the PoolManager send and refuses the transfer back from the fresh address. 48 tokens changed nothing on a one way transfer, which means the transfer from the PoolManager failed inside the stub. I take these to be the paused and frozen tokens of the kind the cycle 4 critic note lists, and did not identify or value them one by one. 44 tokens write a third slot of their own on the way out, and for 36 of them the round trip restores it. 233 tokens failed on the rate limit of the endpoint.',
  'source':'debug_traceCall with stateOverrides on https://sparkling-autumn-dinghy.worldchain-mainnet.quiknode.pro on 2026-11-23, script roundtrip.py and result raw/roundtrip_480.json in this unit\'s work directory'},
 {'chainIds':[480],'lens':'L3','check':'Exits, token transfer cost against the gas cap. MUPPETS on World Chain loops over all holders on every transfer. Can an ordinary account make a take of it impossible, and does a pool depend on it.',
  'result':'An ordinary account can make the token untransferable, and no pool depends on it. The verified source runs distributeDividends on every transfer, with two loops over the holder array and a balance write per holder. With 347 holders a transfer from the PoolManager cost 4,553,179 gas in a simulation, and each of six added holders raised the cost by 14,143 gas. At about 1,210 holders a transfer needs more than 16,777,216 gas and can no longer be included, although the block limit is 280,000,000. Adding the missing 864 holders takes one dust transfer each, about 9.2 billion gas in total, which is about 0.005 ETH at the base fee of 511,203 wei before the L1 data fee. The PoolManager holds 101.39 MUPPETS, and the explorer log search returns no Initialize log with the token as currency0 or currency1 (the same search returns 386 logs for USDC), so the balance is a stray transfer and no position is at stake. The same source credits every holder, the PoolManager included, with a share of a 1 percent fee on each transfer, which is the unsolicited balance growth recorded in F-070.',
  'source':'https://worldchain-mainnet.explorer.alchemy.com/api/v2/smart-contracts/0xd6a4f1f6588ecb8ba3c41ffcb89b1ed185b073ac ; eth_getStorageAt slot 5 and eth_simulateV1 on https://sparkling-autumn-dinghy.worldchain-mainnet.quiknode.pro at block 38,059,166; explorer API module=logs on worldchain-mainnet.explorer.alchemy.com'},
 {'chainIds':[480],'lens':'L3','check':'BB on World Chain, where the PoolManager holds 99 percent of the supply and every transfer adds to two counters in a second contract. Is there a cap that repaid takes can use up.',
  'result':'None below 120 times the PoolManager balance. Sixty transfers of the whole balance to a fresh address and back, in one simulated block, all returned success, the gas per transfer stayed near 90,000, and the PoolManager balance was unchanged at the end. The second contract, 0xd7c91ecad10a33bf6f77f8c24089cad37be7a9f9, has 13,096 bytes and no verified source, so a cap above that size or a limit per day is not excluded.',
  'source':'eth_simulateV1 with state overrides on https://sparkling-autumn-dinghy.worldchain-mainnet.quiknode.pro on 2026-11-23'},
 {'chainIds':[10,130],'lens':'L4','check':'Lead L-352, state today: do the interop predeploys hold implementations on OP Mainnet, Unichain or their Sepolia testnets.',
  'result':'No. The implementation slot is zero at 0x22 to 0x2b and 0x2e to 0x30 on all four networks (OP Mainnet block 159,927,264, Unichain 64,704,949, OP Sepolia 51,825,395, Unichain Sepolia 68,600,905). The registry lists Lagoon as not scheduled and the notice keeps 10 December 2026 and 11 January 2027 as targets. The clearance of cycle 5 from source therefore still describes code that is not deployed anywhere.',
  'source':'eth_getStorageAt on https://mainnet.optimism.io, https://mainnet.unichain.org, https://sepolia.optimism.io and https://sepolia.unichain.org on 2026-11-23; https://docs.optimism.io/op-stack/protocol/network-upgrades ; https://docs.optimism.io/notices/interop-prep'},
]

leads=[
 {'chainIds':ALL,'lens':'L3','question':'For every currency of an initialized pool on the seven chains (the complete lists of cycle 6), what does a transfer of 1 unit from the PoolManager to a fresh address cost in gas, which tokens need more than 1,000,000 gas, and for which of those does the cost grow with a number that any account can raise (holders, checkpoints, registered recipients)?','why':'MUPPETS on World Chain shows the pattern: 4.55 million gas per transfer today, 14,143 more per holder, and no transfer possible above about 1,210 holders because of the 16,777,216 gas cap. That token is in no pool. A pooled token of the same kind would let any account freeze takes of it for a few dollars of gas, and the tests of earlier cycles compared balances and storage and never recorded gas.','priority':'medium'},
 {'chainIds':[10,130],'lens':'L4','question':'Lead L-352 carried forward: after Lagoon activates on OP Sepolia and Unichain Sepolia (target 10 December 2026), which implementations sit behind 0x4200000000000000000000000000000000000022 to 0x28 and 0x30, and what does each return to the sixteen call shapes through a forwarding stub at the PoolManager address?','why':'The slots are empty on all four networks today, so the cycle 5 clearance still rests on source that is not deployed.','priority':'high'},
 {'chainIds':[10,480,7777777],'lens':'L1','question':'Which op-reth revision do the OP Mainnet sequencer, World Chain v2.8.2 and the Conduit build of Zora (conduit-op-reth v2.5.1) run, and does each contain optimism pull request 22778 (bare RLP deposits rejected) and reth pull request 28264 (trie iterator fix)?','why':'Both fixes were tied to release numbers only. A chain without the first fix leaves its batcher able to place an unsigned deposit with a chosen sender, and a chain without the second can stall or serve wrong proofs.','priority':'low'},
 {'chainIds':[480],'lens':'L3','question':'What does the unverified contract 0xd7c91ecad10a33bf6f77f8c24089cad37be7a9f9 do with the two counters that every BB transfer raises, and does it hold a limit per period or a threshold at which transfers of BB 0x4c447f3b26fa2c9d782eeb4d9dd47c7a2d2a52b8 start to revert?','why':'The PoolManager holds 99 percent of the BB supply. Sixty round trips passed, which excludes only a cap below 120 balances. A volume limit above that would let any account stop takes of BB with repaid takes.','priority':'low'},
 {'chainIds':[480],'lens':'L3','question':'Why does PIZZA 0xa1f70fbc0ccb017f0c2e10cd4581c543082fcdcc refuse a transfer from a fresh address that has just received the full amount from the PoolManager, and can a third party put the PoolManager into the same state by sending it a small amount?','why':'If the refusal is a waiting time that starts on receipt, any account could block takes of the token by repeated dust transfers to the PoolManager. The PoolManager holds 170 PIZZA.','priority':'low'},
 {'chainIds':[480,1868],'lens':'L3','question':'Rerun the round trip state diff for the 233 World Chain tokens that failed on the two requests per second limit and for the 18 Soneium tokens that were not reached.','why':'The clearance of lead L-306 covers 457 of 690 tokens on World Chain and 70 of 88 on Soneium.','priority':'low'},
 {'chainIds':[10,8453],'lens':'L3','question':'Simulate the two party AGL sequence on OP Mainnet or Base: a buy that ends with mint, then a sell by a second caller in the same transaction. Does the second payment revert with ForaDaCota as the hook source implies?','why':'The finding of this cycle that extends F-074 rests on the hook source alone.','priority':'low'},
]

leadResults=[
 {'leadId':'L-352','outcome':'inconclusive','answer':'Lagoon is not active on any of the four networks. The implementation slots of the interop predeploys are zero on OP Mainnet, Unichain, OP Sepolia and Unichain Sepolia on 2026-11-23, and the registry lists Lagoon as not scheduled. The question can be answered after the Sepolia activation targeted for 10 December 2026.'},
 {'leadId':'L-377','outcome':'cleared','answer':'Unichain: one transaction, 0x2b6aab1368b9266b585e655377e5a9431b822e4ff4657fec34166824e372a82e in block 60,794,133, sent by 0x053d984eab3240cdf7c53b8b7846159f8146e99b through 0xec9a66a21a9c201bafa9a218b716403a27480409. In it the hook mints 0.01 units of id 0x29f2f95c696e93939d8ce77819377d16bd0dcc04 and burns 0.01 units of the UNI id, which is the whole 0.01 UNI difference. It is one use of the payout step that F-084 describes and not a series. Ethereum: the public endpoint returned no receipt for 0xd903d0ada1874646896a5191a2ef25accec3c3db1333914542b6ad9a8c96bfed, so the Ethereum half rests on the cycle 5 verdict and on F-096.'},
 {'leadId':'L-484','outcome':'cleared','answer':'The client source at v1.5.6 was read for the stablecoin and asset logic v3, both dispatchers, the factory and the policy registry. Every balance write needs the owner as caller, an allowance from the owner, a token role, or the factory during creation of that same token. transfer credits the named recipient or reverts. Policy membership and policy admin change only on a call of the policy admin. Nineteen unprivileged calls against OUSD and the registry with the PoolManager as holder all reverted or returned zero at block 61,314,282. One issuer level fact is new and is filed under F-027: the block lists bound to OUSD and AMDc are each administered by one account without code.'},
 {'leadId':'L-265','outcome':'cleared','answer':'Answered from the whole Initialize history that theme-shared-hooks read in cycle 5, which is wider than the 500,000 blocks asked for. Base: ZoraV4CoinHook 0xc8d077444625eb300a427a6dfb2b1dbf9b159040 (8,189,715 pools; afterInitialize, afterSwap), ContentCoinHook 0x9ea932730a7787000042e34390b8e435dd839040 (1,895,318; same flags), DecayMulticurveInitializerHook 0xbb7784a4d481184283ed89619a3e3ed143e1adc0 (987,058; no return delta flag), ZoraV4CoinHook 0x0469a4bd3724dc86c9542f4694c976da13c450c0 (951,206; no return delta flag) and ClankerHookStaticFeeV2 0xb429d62f8f3bffb98cdb9569533ea23bf0ba28cc (651,626; beforeSwap and afterSwap return delta). Unichain: ClankerHookStaticFee 0xbc6e5abda425309c2534bc2bc92562f5419ce8cc (1,310; both swap return delta flags), PrediXHookProxyV2 0x2ea5eac8a4e31f0889e86fc135c5eae8e0b16ae0 (1,034; none), UniMemeHook 0xb4960cd4f9147f9e37a7aa9005df7156f61e4444 (777; afterSwap return delta), 0x782d2a5624cfac246fd8f001ccd4d7582a0b8444 (166; afterSwap return delta) and UniderpHook 0xcc2efb167503f2d7df0eae906600066aec9e8444 (114; afterSwap return delta). Zora: 11 of 73 pools name a hook, no hook has more than two pools, and all five listed carry the afterSwap return delta flag. Ethereum, Arbitrum One and BNB Smart Chain are in the same cycle 5 items. On Base only one of the five most used hooks can take from the swapper\'s delta, and it refuses pools it did not create.'},
 {'leadId':'L-304','outcome':'finding','answer':'From the verified PoolAGL source: the quota is one transient value that afterSwap changes and token transfers consume, and nothing resets it between unlocks. A party that buys AGL and closes with mint leaves a negative quota. A later seller in the same transaction then fails in consumirEntrada, a later buyer after a seller who paid with burned claims fails in consumirSaida, and a later holder of AGL claims can burn and take against the leftover quota without a swap. The effect is a reverted operation for the later party and no transfer of value. Filed as an extension of F-074 with actor pool-creator and severity low. The sequence was not simulated on chain.'},
 {'leadId':'L-306','outcome':'cleared','answer':'World Chain: a QuickNode endpoint serves debug_traceCall with state overrides. 457 tokens were traced, and no per sender counter, cooldown or volume cap was found that a repaid take uses up. BB writes two counters in an unverified contract and passed sixty round trips. Zora and Soneium have no public trace endpoint. A substitute from eth_createAccessList and eth_simulateV1 gave state diffs for 41 and 70 tokens with no counter found. Celo and Arc are outside this unit and were not attempted. The simulate method works on any endpoint that serves the two calls and can be used there.'},
]

out={'unit':'op-stack-eth','_lenses':['L1','L2','L3','L4'],'profiles':profiles,'findings':findings,'cleared':cleared,'leads':leads,'leadResults':leadResults}
json.dump(out,open(OUT,'w'),indent=1,ensure_ascii=False)
print('profiles',len(profiles),'findings',len(findings),'cleared',len(cleared),'leads',len(leads))
# style check
import re
txt=json.dumps(out,ensure_ascii=False)
for bad in ['—','–','crucial','robust','landscape','highlight','underscore','**']:
    if bad in txt: print('STYLE HIT',repr(bad),txt.count(bad))
for m in re.finditer(r'\bkey\b',txt,flags=re.I): print('KEY at',txt[max(0,m.start()-50):m.end()+30])
for m in re.finditer(r'\bnot\b[^.]{0,60}\bbut\b',txt): print('NOT-BUT',txt[max(0,m.start()-30):m.end()+30])
